WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.616
<v HOST>Welcome back to Grace Notes.

00:00:01.966 --> 00:00:06.536
<v HOST>I'm Maya Chen, and today we are talking about forgiveness with Pastor Owen Hartley.

00:00:06.886 --> 00:00:08.702
<v HOST>Owen, thanks for joining me.

00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:11.199
<v GUEST>Thanks for having me, Maya.

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:13.590
<v GUEST>This topic is close to my heart.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:19.593
<v HOST>Years ago, I had only been at that job one day when a coworker presented my idea as his own.

00:00:19.943 --> 00:00:24.258
<v HOST>It kept happening for months, and I carried that resentment for a long time.

00:00:24.608 --> 00:00:26.074
<v HOST>Why is forgiveness so hard?

00:00:26.674 --> 00:00:28.127
<v GUEST>Because the hurt is real.

00:00:28.477 --> 00:00:31.117
<v GUEST>Forgiveness is not pretending that nothing happened.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:36.392
<v GUEST>It is choosing to release a debt that someone owes you, even when they have not paid it back.

00:00:36.992 --> 00:00:37.836
<v HOST>Release a debt.

00:00:38.186 --> 00:00:40.877
<v HOST>That language shows up in prayer too, doesn't it?

00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:42.107
<v GUEST>It does.

00:00:42.457 --> 00:00:44.585
<v GUEST>Many of us say these words every week.

00:00:44.935 --> 00:00:48.276
<v GUEST>Forgive us our debts, as we also forgive our debtors.

00:00:48.626 --> 00:00:53.122
<v GUEST>Every time we pray that line, we tie the mercy we receive to the mercy we give.

00:00:53.722 --> 00:00:55.991
<v HOST>But what if the same person keeps hurting you?

00:00:56.341 --> 00:00:57.150
<v HOST>Is there a limit?

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:02.801
<v GUEST>Peter asked that exact question, in Matthew eighteen, twenty-one and twenty-two.

00:01:03.151 --> 00:01:09.431
<v GUEST>He wants to know how often he should forgive a brother who keeps sinning against him, and he suggests seven times.

00:01:09.781 --> 00:01:14.254
<v GUEST>Jesus answers, not seven times, but seventy times seven.

00:01:14.854 --> 00:01:18.062
<v HOST>So Jesus isn't handing us a bigger number to count toward.

00:01:18.662 --> 00:01:19.431
<v GUEST>Exactly.

00:01:19.781 --> 00:01:22.068
<v GUEST>He is telling us to stop keeping score.

00:01:22.418 --> 00:01:25.742
<v GUEST>Forgiveness becomes a way of life instead of a transaction.

00:01:26.092 --> 00:01:28.667
<v GUEST>That does not mean we ignore wisdom or safety.

00:01:29.017 --> 00:01:32.423
<v GUEST>Forgiving someone and trusting them again are two different things.

00:01:33.023 --> 00:01:36.870
<v HOST>Let's talk about the story most people picture when they hear the word grace.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:38.550
<v GUEST>The prodigal son.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:44.093
<v GUEST>A younger son takes his share of the family wealth, leaves home, and wastes all of it.

00:01:44.443 --> 00:01:47.159
<v GUEST>He ends up feeding pigs, hungry and alone.

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:52.928
<v GUEST>Finally he heads home, practicing a speech about becoming one of his father's hired workers.

00:01:53.528 --> 00:01:56.056
<v HOST>And the father never lets him finish that speech.

00:01:56.656 --> 00:01:57.135
<v GUEST>Right.

00:01:57.485 --> 00:02:03.624
<v GUEST>The father spots him while he is still far away, runs to him, hugs him, and throws a party.

00:02:03.974 --> 00:02:07.283
<v GUEST>The son expected a lecture, and he got a celebration.

00:02:07.633 --> 00:02:09.952
<v GUEST>That is what the forgiveness of God looks like.

00:02:10.552 --> 00:02:12.867
<v HOST>But there's an older brother in that story too.

00:02:13.467 --> 00:02:17.278
<v GUEST>Yes, and honestly, many church people are more like him.

00:02:17.628 --> 00:02:21.644
<v GUEST>He stayed home, worked hard, and resented his father's mercy.

00:02:21.994 --> 00:02:27.683
<v GUEST>The story ends with the father begging him to join the celebration, and we never find out what he decided.

00:02:28.283 --> 00:02:30.410
<v HOST>So where does someone actually begin?

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.287
<v GUEST>Start with how much you have been forgiven.

00:02:33.637 --> 00:02:36.516
<v GUEST>Paul says it simply in Ephesians four thirty-two.

00:02:36.866 --> 00:02:43.115
<v GUEST>Be kind to one another, tender hearted, forgiving each other, just as God also in Christ forgave you.

00:02:43.465 --> 00:02:45.076
<v GUEST>The key words are just as.

00:02:45.426 --> 00:02:48.552
<v GUEST>We forgive out of the overflow of what we have already received.

00:02:49.152 --> 00:02:52.953
<v HOST>Owen, any last word for someone who is carrying an old hurt today?

00:02:53.553 --> 00:02:54.909
<v GUEST>Take one small step.

00:02:55.259 --> 00:02:59.253
<v GUEST>Pray for that person by name, and ask God to help you release the debt.

00:02:59.603 --> 00:03:02.752
<v GUEST>You may need to do it again tomorrow, and that is okay.

00:03:03.352 --> 00:03:04.538
<v HOST>Thank you, Owen.

00:03:04.888 --> 00:03:06.916
<v HOST>And thanks for listening to Grace Notes.
